### Scenario

You want to:

* ✅ Keep a folder/files on your local machine
* ✅ Remove them from GitHub
* ✅ Prevent them from being committed again

### Steps

```bash
# 1. Remove folder from Git tracking only
git rm -r --cached <folder_name>

# 2. Add folder to .gitignore
echo "<folder_name>/" >> .gitignore

# 3. Stage .gitignore
git add .gitignore

# 4. Commit the change
git commit -m "Remove <folder_name> from repository"

# 5. Get latest changes from GitHub
git pull --rebase origin main

# 6. Push changes
git push origin main
```

### Example

```bash
git rm -r --cached English

echo "English/" >> .gitignore

git add .gitignore

git commit -m "Remove English folder from repository"

git pull --rebase origin main

git push origin main
```

### Verify

```bash
git status
```

Expected:

```text
On branch main
nothing to commit, working tree clean
```

### If `git push` is rejected

```bash
git pull --rebase origin main
git push origin main
```

### If you accidentally run `git rm -r <folder_name>` (without `--cached`)

Before committing:

```bash
git restore --staged <folder_name>
git restore <folder_name>
```

This is the standard workflow I recommend for removing personal notes, PDFs, local configs, `.terraform`, lab files, etc. from GitHub while keeping them on your machine.
